  "title": {
    "template": "title",
    "heading": "The multi-store model of memory",
    "lead": "Recall: why might you forget a new number but remember your home address?",
    "picture": null
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "template": "big-visual",
      "heading": "Three stores, different properties",
      "lead": "Coding is the form of information; capacity is how much fits; duration is how long it lasts.",
      "figure": {
        "kind": "table",
        "shows": "Typical A-level estimates for the three stores in Atkinson and Shiffrin's model",
        "alt": "The sensory register is brief and high capacity, STM is limited and mainly acoustic, and LTM is lasting and mainly semantic.",
        "title": null,
        "header": ["Store", "Coding", "Capacity", "Duration"],
        "rows": [
          ["Sensory register", "Modality-specific", "Very large", "Usually under 2 s"],
          ["STM", "Mainly acoustic", "7 ± 2 items", "About 18–30 s"],
          ["LTM", "Mainly semantic", "Potentially unlimited", "Potentially lifelong"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "How information moves",
      "lead": "Atkinson and Shiffrin proposed separate stores linked by control processes.",
      "points": [
        "Attention selects sensory information for STM.",
        "Maintenance rehearsal repeats information in STM.",
        "Retrieval brings stored information from LTM to STM."
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "flow",
        "shows": "The multi-store model, including the rehearsal loop and transfer to long-term memory",
        "alt": "Attention links the sensory register to STM; rehearsal loops in STM and supports transfer to LTM; retrieval returns information to STM.",
        "title": null,
        "nodes": ["Sensory register", "STM", "LTM"],
        "links": [
          {
            "from": 0,
            "to": 1,
            "label": "Attention"
          },
          {
            "from": 1,
            "to": 1,
            "label": "Rehearsal"
          },
          {
            "from": 1,
            "to": 2,
            "label": "Prolonged rehearsal"
          },
          {
            "from": 2,
            "to": 1,
            "label": "Retrieval"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "Worked example: an access code",
      "lead": "Amir hears a code, repeats it aloud and later recalls it without hearing it again.",
      "points": [
        "Attention selects the sounds for mainly acoustic STM.",
        "Rehearsal keeps the code active and supports LTM storage.",
        "Later recall retrieves the code from LTM into STM."
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "flow",
        "shows": "The access-code example mapped onto the model",
        "alt": "The heard code enters the sensory register, attention carries it to STM, rehearsal supports LTM storage, and later retrieval returns it to STM.",
        "title": null,
        "nodes": ["Heard code: sensory", "Repeated code: STM", "Stored code: LTM"],
        "links": [
          {
            "from": 0,
            "to": 1,
            "label": "Attention"
          },
          {
            "from": 1,
            "to": 1,
            "label": "Rehearsal"
          },
          {
            "from": 1,
            "to": 2,
            "label": "Prolonged rehearsal"
          },
          {
            "from": 2,
            "to": 1,
            "label": "Retrieval"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "practice",
      "heading": "Explain a new code",
      "questions": [
        "Leah hears a locker code and focuses on it. Explain how it reaches STM.",
        "She repeats the code aloud for a while. Explain its coding and the role of repetition.",
        "Next week, she recalls it without hearing it again. Explain which stores and process are involved."
      ],
      "instruction": "Write one or two sentences for each stage.",
      "picture": null
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "Repetition is not a guarantee",
      "lead": "Keeping information active now is not the same as remembering it next week.",
      "points": [
        {
          "label": "Model prediction",
          "text": "Prolonged rehearsal supports transfer to LTM."
        },
        {
          "label": "Avoid the shortcut",
          "text": "One repetition does not guarantee lasting storage."
        }
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "flow",
        "shows": "A number repeated briefly remains active in STM but may be lost when rehearsal stops",
        "alt": "A briefly repeated number loops in STM; if rehearsal stops, it may be lost rather than becoming a lasting memory.",
        "title": null,
        "nodes": ["Number in STM", "Rehearsal stops", "May be forgotten"],
        "links": [
          {
            "from": 0,
            "to": 0,
            "label": "Brief repetition"
          },
          {
            "from": 0,
            "to": 1,
            "label": null
          },
          {
            "from": 1,
            "to": 2,
            "label": null
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "hinge",
      "heading": "Choose the best explanation",
      "stem": "A number is repeated aloud for 10 seconds. What does the model predict?",
      "options": [
        "It is maintained in mainly acoustic STM.",
        "It remains in the sensory register throughout.",
        "It is guaranteed to remain in LTM for life.",
        "Repetition retrieves it from LTM every time."
      ],
      "correct": 1
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "Glanzer and Cunitz: serial position",
      "lead": "Word-list recall was compared immediately and after a filled delay that prevented rehearsal.",
      "points": [
        {
          "label": "Primacy",
          "text": "Early words: more rehearsal and LTM storage."
        },
        {
          "label": "Recency",
          "text": "Final words: available in STM at immediate recall."
        }
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "line-graph",
        "shows": "Schematic serial position curves: early-word recall remains relatively high after a filled delay, but the final-word advantage is reduced",
        "alt": "Both curves show good early-word recall. Immediate recall also shows good final-word recall, while delayed recall loses much of that final-word advantage.",
        "title": null,
        "x": {
          "label": "Position in word list",
          "min": 1,
          "max": 10,
          "step": 1
        },
        "y": {
          "label": "Recall",
          "min": 0,
          "max": 10,
          "step": 2
        },
        "y2": null,
        "qualitative": true,
        "series": [
          {
            "label": "Immediate",
            "points": [[1, 8], [2, 7], [3, 5], [4, 4], [5, 3], [6, 3], [7, 4], [8, 5], [9, 7], [10, 9]],
            "style": "line",
            "axis": "left"
          },
          {
            "label": "Filled delay",
            "points": [[1, 8], [2, 7], [3, 5], [4, 4], [5, 3], [6, 3], [7, 3], [8, 3], [9, 3], [10, 3]],
            "style": "line",
            "axis": "left"
          }
        ],
        "segments": null,
        "annotations": null,
        "intervals": null
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "KF: is STM one store?",
      "lead": "Shallice and Warrington studied KF, who had brain damage after a motorcycle accident.",
      "points": [
        "His verbal STM was severely impaired, especially for heard material.",
        "His visual STM was relatively preserved.",
        "Different impairments challenge one uniform STM store."
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "table",
        "shows": "The contrast between KF's verbal and visual short-term memory",
        "alt": "KF had severely impaired verbal STM, especially for auditory material, but relatively preserved visual STM.",
        "title": null,
        "header": ["STM task", "KF's performance"],
        "rows": [
          ["Verbal", "Severely impaired"],
          ["Visual", "Better preserved"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "steps",
      "heading": "Worked evaluation: supporting evidence",
      "points": [
        "Claim: serial position findings support separate STM and LTM stores.",
        "Evidence: a filled delay reduced recency but left primacy relatively intact.",
        "Link: delay affects recent STM items more than rehearsed LTM items; this supports, but does not prove, separate stores."
      ],
      "figure": null
    },
    {
      "template": "practice",
      "heading": "Evaluate and apply",
      "questions": [
        "KF had impaired verbal STM but better-preserved visual STM. Write a claim–evidence–link paragraph challenging one STM store.",
        "A class recalls a word list immediately or after counting backwards for 30 seconds. Predict how recall of early and final words will differ. Explain why.",
        "Stretch: could the class result prove every part of the model? Explain one reason for caution."
      ],
      "instruction": "Use precise store and process names.",
      "picture": null
    }
  ]
}
